  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   The central idea is to use a combined optimization strategy that combines continuous optimization with a combinatorial relaxation of the problem. We view the overlap minimization as a minimum weighted clique cover in a graph, where vertices are positions and edges represent overlapping regions. Our algorithm will:\n   - Start with a locally optimal Fourier-initialized solution\n   - Solve a convex relaxation using coordinate descent\n   - Apply a graph-based reduction technique to handle combinatorial structure\n   - Iterate between coarse-grained optimization and fine-grained refinement\n   \n   This addresses the bottleneck of local optimality by introducing combinatorial structure. The limitations are:\n   - Fourier initialization may not capture all patterns\n   - Convex relaxation may yield fractional solutions\n   - Graph cover may not map exactly to continuous space constraints\n   \n   We will use aknown Fourier basis as initial point, then employ Noptimizer to enforce fractional solutions to the clique cover problem, finally projecting back to the continuous space while preserving the minimal overlap.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Let f(h,k) = \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx for k\u2208[-(n-1),n-1] be the overlap metric\n   - The objective minimizes C5 = max_k f(h,k) subject to:\n        \u2211h_i = n/2\n        0\u2264h_i\u22641\n   - We form a weighted graph G where:\n        V = {0,1,...,n-1}\n        E = {(i,j) | i-j=r for some r\u2208E_R}\n        Weight w_{i,j} = f(|i-j|) / (n*dx) for the normalized cost\n   \n   - The clique cover problem is formulated as:\n        min \u2211_{cliques} x_c * (sum_{i\u2208c} w_i) + M * \u2211_c x_c * y_c\n        where y_c indicates whether vertex i\u2208c\n        subject to y_c \u2264 y_{c+1} for nested cliques\n        \u2211_{c} (y_i) = n\n        0\u2264y_i\u22641\n   \n   - This is relaxed to a continuous program:\n        minimize t\n        subject to \u2211_{i,j,k} |i-j+k-x_m| h_m \u2265 -t (scaled constraints)\n        \u2211h_m = n/2\n        0\u2264h_m\u22641\n        t \u2265 0.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Initialization (step 1): Use Fourier decomposition to generate h0\n        h0[i] = 0.5 + 0.2 * sin(2\u03c0i/3 + 4\u03c6_i + K/2)\n        with \u03c6_i uniformly random\n        then project to [0,1]\n   - Coordinate Descent (step 2): Perform multiple CD iterations\n        while n<5*initial_n:\n            merge adjacent indices every 10 steps\n            clip probability increases by factor 5 after decay\n            objective for neighbor search: grid-based gradient\n            dimensionality reduction via attention-focused sampling\n        update neighbor selection via softmax focusing on highest contribution\n   - Graph Processing (step 3):\n        1. Compute the continuous embedding G = factor(\u03a3(x,cost(x)))\n        2. Apply kernel PCA with \u03c3 = (sample spacing)\n        3. Cluster vertices using k-medoids with k=20\n        4. Transform constraints to separable form via Lagrangian duality\n   - Robust Optimization (step 4):\n        split solution space topology using Reber-6 classifier\n        use gradient-free methods for noisy regions\n        embed primal-dual into Chebyshev net\n        handle warm-starts via solution archiving\n\n4. Search Choices and Use Variations\n   The core variation is the selection of convolution kernel size K in Fourier initialization. We consider K=15,35,60 as options. Other refinements include:\n   - Coarse-graining iterations: 100-200 per phase\n   - Neighbor search: uniform vs. Pareto-centric\n   - Clustering method: k-medoids vs. spectral\n   - Objective priority: QAP style auto-deduction of kernel weights\n   \n   Each change is triggered by stagnation detection (10 iterations with no improvement) or decreasing interval (when feasible solutions are dense). \n\n5. Budget Estimation\n   The approach scales as O(n^{1.5}) however using sparse-graph techniques we achieve O(n log n) wall-clock. For n=600:\n   - Initialization: ~15 s\n   - Coordinate descent: 500 iterations \u00d7 0.5 s = 250 s\n   - Graph processing: 40 s\n   - Optimization: 20 s\n   Total ~425 s < 1100 s budget.\n\n   Checkpoints occur after 100 iterations of CD, and terminated if no progress in 20 CD steps. Memory requirement is ~2GB for n=600.\n\n6. Validation and Contract\n   - Check feasibility: solution in [0,1] and sum= n/2\n   - Re-evaluate C5 using same correlation method\n   - Ensure objective derivative is bounded\n   - Output format: (h_values.astype(float32), c5_bound, len(h_values))\n   - Requires fallback if solution invalid: use median projection",
